,"circuit_facts":[{"label":"kicked_ising_theta_0.0pi","cz_count":249,"measured_qubits":["QB1","QB2","QB3","QB4","QB5","QB6","QB7","QB8","QB9","QB10","QB11","QB12","QB13","QB14","QB15","QB16","QB17","QB18","QB19","QB20","QB21","QB22","QB23","QB24","QB25","QB26","QB27","QB28","QB29","QB30","QB31","QB32","QB33","QB34","QB35","QB36","QB37","QB38","QB39","QB40","QB41","QB42","QB43","QB44","QB45","QB46","QB47","QB48","QB49","QB50","QB51","QB52","QB53","QB54"]},{"label":"kicked_ising_theta_0.2pi","cz_count":249,"measured_qubits":["QB1","QB2","QB3","QB4","QB5","QB6","QB7","QB8","QB9","QB10","QB11","QB12","QB13","QB14","QB15","QB16","QB17","QB18","QB19","QB20","QB21","QB22","QB23","QB24","QB25","QB26","QB27","QB28","QB29","QB30","QB31","QB32","QB33","QB34","QB35","QB36","QB37","QB38","QB39","QB40","QB41","QB42","QB43","QB44","QB45","QB46","QB47","QB48","QB49","QB50","QB51","QB52","QB53","QB54"]},{"label":"kicked_ising_theta_0.35pi","cz_count":249,"measured_qubits":["QB1","QB2","QB3","QB4","QB5","QB6","QB7","QB8","QB9","QB10","QB11","QB12","QB13","QB14","QB15","QB16","QB17","QB18","QB19","QB20","QB21","QB22","QB23","QB24","QB25","QB26","QB27","QB28","QB29","QB30","QB31","QB32","QB33","QB34","QB35","QB36","QB37","QB38","QB39","QB40","QB41","QB42","QB43","QB44","QB45","QB46","QB47","QB48","QB49","QB50","QB51","QB52","QB53","QB54"]},{"label":"kicked_ising_theta_0.5pi","cz_count":249,"measured_qubits":["QB1","QB2","QB3","QB4","QB5","QB6","QB7","QB8","QB9","QB10","QB11","QB12","QB13","QB14","QB15","QB16","QB17","QB18","QB19","QB20","QB21","QB22","QB23","QB24","QB25","QB26","QB27","QB28","QB29","QB30","QB31","QB32","QB33","QB34","QB35","QB36","QB37","QB38","QB39","QB40","QB41","QB42","QB43","QB44","QB45","QB46","QB47","QB48","QB49","QB50","QB51","QB52","QB53","QB54"]}],"answer":"I ran the experiment as one job on emerald:mock, IQM's free Emerald test device. The job ID is 01a16484-7c69-7062-89c7-052674140b5b. It had 4 circuits with 250 shots each, and it cost 0 credits. The API reported billed_seconds 1.0, but mock jobs are not charged.\n\nWhat I built: Each circuit uses all 54 qubits, one per atom. Every qubit starts at 0. One time step is a sideways field kick, RX(theta), on every qubit. Then every coupler gets one RZZ(-pi/2) = exp(i pi/4 ZZ) gate. I used the coupler list that the real emerald reports now (calibration set 3a1a9ad0-0c98-49db-8cf5-7623d13729f4). That list has 83 couplers. The mock lists 90, and those 90 include all 83 real ones. I left out the 7 extra mock-only couplers, because you asked for the couplers the real chip lists. On IQM hardware each RZZ(-pi/2) is exactly one CZ gate plus Z rotations. The Z rotations cost nothing and are folded into the phases of the later RX pulses. I wrote the circuits in IQM's native gate format and sent them without any compiler, so nothing could add, merge, or remove a coupler gate. I read the circuits back from the server. Each one has exactly 249 CZ gates, which is 3 steps times 83 couplers. Each one measures QB1 to QB54. I checked the gate rewrite against the textbook model on a 12-qubit patch, and the results agreed to 1e-15.\n\nMagnetization is the average of Z over the 54 qubits. A qubit that reads 0 counts +1, and a qubit that reads 1 counts -1.\n\nIdeal values: I computed these with an exact noise-free simulation. After 3 steps, each qubit can only feel qubits up to 2 couplers away. That makes an exact calculation possible for all 54 qubits.\n- theta = 0: ideal 1.000, measured -0.005\n- theta = 0.2 pi: ideal 0.446, measured 0.031\n- theta = 0.35 pi: ideal 0.102, measured -0.006\n- theta = 0.5 pi: ideal 0.000, measured 0.002\n\nWhat a perfect quantum computer would show: With no field (theta = 0), nothing tips the spins. They all stay up, so the magnetization stays at 1. A stronger sideways field tips each spin further from up. The spin-spin coupling then spreads and scrambles those tips across the sheet. So the magnetization falls from 1.0 to 0.45 to 0.10, and it reaches 0 at theta = 0.5 pi. At 0.5 pi each kick is a quarter turn. After three of them, the ideal sheet has no net up or down at all. This is the melting you wanted to see. The field puts the spins into quantum superpositions of up and down, and their entanglement with neighbors wipes out the order.\n\nWhat the test device showed: All four measured values are near 0, about plus or minus 0.03. The mock does not simulate the circuit. It returns random bitstrings. That is why even theta = 0 reads about 0 and not 1. The mock run shows that the job is valid and is accepted for the full 54-qubit chip with the current coupler list. It cannot show the physics. To see the melting curve in real data, the same 4 circuits would need to run on the real emerald. That costs credits at about 0.75 credits per second, so I did not run it without your OK. counts holds the final circuit (theta = 0.5 pi), with qubit QB1 as the leftmost character."}
